/*!
	\page tutorial.html tutorial
	\title Bullet Hell Game Tutorial using Felgo

	\section1 Introduction

	This tutorial describes how to create a simple 2D Bullet Hell Game using Felgo.

	The tutorial will provide a base for the overall game, enabling anyone to further expand on it.
	Here we will go in details on how to implement the player controls and shooting mechanics,
	enemies, their state machines and attack patterns, wave spawning and scene management.

	The source code for the finished project is available on \l {https://github.com/Anton3311/felgo-challenge} {GitHub}

	\section1 Assets

	The art assets used throughout this tutorial are from Kenney and are available \l {https://kenney.nl/assets/tiny-dungeon} {here} under CC0 1.0 License. This asset pack doesn't cover all of the needed art, thus some of the missing pieces were hand-drawn in the same style. All of the used art assets are available in GitHub Repository under \l {https://github.com/Anton3311/felgo-challenge/tree/main/assets/entities} {assets/entities}

	The pixel font used in the game is available on \l {https://fonts.google.com/specimen/Jersey+10?query=Jersey+10&preview.script=Latn} {Google Fonts}

	\section1 Project Setup

	Start by creating a new \b{Felgo Games} project in Qt Creator. Sepcifically we will need
	\b{New - Empty Felgo Project} template. The created project will already contain some example code in the \c{Main.qml}, which we will reduce down to these 15:

	\qml
	import Felgo 4.0
	import QtQuick 2.0

	GameWindow {
		id: gameWindow
		activeScene: scene
		screenWidth: 960
		screenHeight: 640

		Scene {
			id: scene
		}
	}
	\endqml

	\section1 Player Controls

	As the first major step, we will start with the player controller, to get at least something
	moving on the screen. But before we dive into coding a player control, we need to prepare out
	scene.

	Firstly our player is an entity, and to be able to work with entities, our scene needs an entity
	manager. So we add one:

	\code
	// This `Item` serves as a container for all entities. Any entities created during the game,
	// will be added to this container.
	Item {
		id: level
	}

	EntityManager {
		id: entityManager
		entityContainer: level
	}
	\endcode

	Now that, we have an entity manager, we can start creating our player.

	The code for the player will live in \c{qml/entities/Player.qml}.

	\qml
	import QtQuick
	import Felgo

	EntityBase {
		id: player
		entityId: "entity"
		entityType: "player"

		Image {
			id: sprite
			// Place the player sprite right in the center of the entity
			anchors.centerIn: parent.anchors.center
			// Disable smoothing to keep the pixel art crisp.
			smooth: false
			source: Qt.resolvedUrl("../../assets/entities/player.png")
			width: 32
			height: 32
		}
	}
	\endqml

	And then in our \c{Main.qml} we will first import the code from entities folder:
	\code
	import Felgo 4.0
	import QtQuick 2.0
	import "./entities"
	\endcode

	Finally add our \c{Player} right after the \c{EntityManager}, like this:
	\qml
	Scene {
		id: scene

		EntityManager {
			id: entityManager
			entityContainer: level
		}

		Player {}
	}
	\endqml

	Now lets move onto player controls. Out will rely on physics, that means we first need to setup a \c{PhysicsWorld}:

	\qml
	Scene {
		id: scene

		EntityManager {
			id: entityManager
			entityContainer: level
		}

		PhysicsWorld {
			id: physicsWorld
			gravity.y: 0
			updatesPerSecondForPhysics: 60
			velocityIterations: 5
			positionIterations: 5
			debugDrawVisible: false
		}

		Player {}
	}
	\endqml

	Now that we have the \c{PhysicsWorld}, we can add a collider to our player. We will also make
	our player sprite a child of the collider. The resulting player code looks like this:

	\qml
	CircleCollider {
		id: collider
		radius: 16
		// Disable rotations caused by collisions.
		fixedRotation: true

		Image {
			id: sprite
			anchors.centerIn: parent.anchors.center
			smooth: false
			source: Qt.resolvedUrl("../../assets/entities/player.png")
			width: 32
			height: 32
		}
	}
	\endqml

	The final step before player is able to move around the scene, is the actual keyboard controls.
	Here we will use a \c{TwoAxisController} with our 4 movement directions mapped to WASD keys.

	\c{TwoAxisController} has two property we are interested in: \c{xAxis} and \c{yAxis}.

	We can use them to provide a velocity vector for the \c{CircleCollider} like this:

	\code
	linearVelocity: Qt.point(axisController.xAxis, axisController.yAxis)
	\endcode

	\c{TwoAxisController} is only able to notify us about, whenever a button gets pressed or release.
	However, we want to be able to presss and hold a button to move in a desired direction. This
	requires a bit more bookkeeping. We will need to keep track of which of the 4-direction keys are
	currently pressed and update that state on each press/release together with manually updating
	\c{xAxis} and \c{yAxis} properties of the controller.

	Add the \c{bool} properties, corresponding each of the movement directions, at the start of the
	\c{Player} entity:

	\code
	property bool isMovingLeft
	property bool isMovingRight
	property bool isMovingUp
	property bool isMovingDown
	\endcode

	Out \c{TwoAxisController} we will lok like this:

	\qml
	TwoAxisController {
		id: axisController
		onInputActionPressed: (actionName) => {
			updateActionState(actionName, true);
			updateMovementAxis();
		}
		onInputActionReleased: (actionName) => {
			updateActionState(actionName, false);
			updateMovementAxis();
		}
		inputActionsToKeyCode: {
			"up": Qt.Key_W,
			"down": Qt.Key_S,
			"left": Qt.Key_A,
			"right": Qt.Key_D
		}
	}
	\endqml

	Also don't forget to add the \c{speed} property to the player entity:

	\code
	property int speed: 150
	\endcode

	And finally the helper functions for state tracking and updating controller properties:

	\code
	function updateActionState(actionName, value) {
		if (actionName === "up") {
			isMovingUp = value
		}

		if (actionName === "down") {
			isMovingDown = value
		}

		if (actionName === "left") {
			isMovingLeft = value
		}

		if (actionName === "right") {
			isMovingRight = value
		}
	}

	function updateMovementAxis() {
		let dx = 0;
		let dy = 0;

		if (player.isMovingUp) {
			dy -= 1;
		}

		if (player.isMovingDown) {
			dy += 1;
		}

		if (player.isMovingLeft) {
			dx -= 1;
		}

		if (player.isMovingRight) {
			dx += 1;
		}

		if (dx === 0 && dy === 0) {
			axisController.xAxis = 0;
			axisController.yAxis = 0;
		} else {
			let deltaVectorLength = Math.sqrt(dx * dx + dy * dy);
			let deltaScale = 1 / deltaVectorLength * player.speed;

			axisController.xAxis = dx * deltaScale;
			axisController.yAxis = dy * deltaScale;
		}
	}
	\endcode

	One more final touch. We can set the \c{mirror} property of the \c{Image} to be computed based
	on the horiontal speed, so the player sprite gets mirrored when the player moves left.

	\code
	mirror: collider.linearVelocity.x < 0
	\endcode

	For the whole player controller to work, we also need to forward all of the key events from the
	scene to our \c{TwoAxisController}. For that we will first need expose the \c{TwoAxisController}
	inside the player to an outside world, by creating an \c{alias property}:

	\code
	EntityBase {
		id: player
		entityId: "entity"
		entityType: "player"

		property int speed: 150
		property bool isMovingLeft
		property bool isMovingRight
		property bool isMovingUp
		property bool isMovingDown

		// Expose the controller to an outside world
		property alias controller: axisController
		...
	\endcode

	And forward the events by adding this code in our \c{Main.qml}:

	\code
	Keys.forwardTo: [player.controller]

	Player {
		id: player
	}
	\endcode

	At this point the player should be able to move around the screen.

*/
